#!/bin/bash
# guard: the auto-mode guard, one run. Run by <name>-automode-guard.service
# every 15 minutes (its timer), as the instance's user, outside Claude Code,
# so no Claude Code setting can switch it off, and so the agent's own session
# never edits its settings file. It watches Claude Code's auto-mode rules
# (~/.claude/settings.json: autoMode.allow, soft_deny, hard_deny) for the
# instance's owner, and asks the owner in plain words, on Buzz, about three
# kinds of change:
#   - rules not on record ("unmade"): in the live settings, not in the
#     snapshot (as committed on the instance repo's main), and not signed in
#     the ledger;
#   - rules on record that are gone: in the snapshot, or signed in the ledger
#     and in force, and missing from the live settings, unless the ledger has
#     a remove line for them;
#   - rules that expired but are still live: in the live settings, not in the
#     snapshot, and the latest ledger line for them an add whose expiry has
#     passed, or does not parse (an invalid expiry, never "never").
# A change signed in the ledger and in force (an add with no expiry or one
# still to come, or a remove) is not reported at all. "$defaults" (Claude
# Code's own default rules) is never asked about as added, and no ledger
# line counts for it; its removal from a list that had it, in the snapshot or
# in the live settings as the guard saw them before (the state keeps those
# list names), is always asked about as gone, and Put back puts it back.
#
# The three messages ("them" for one rule is "it"). The first is the owner's
# approved words. The other two quote each rule (cut to fit) and say where it
# is on record, never who made it: the snapshot and the ledger are files the
# instance's user can write (see read_ledger).
#   I found 2 rules you didn't make. Another agent may have added them; I'm
#   not sure. What would you like to do?
#   • Show me them  • Keep them  • Remove them
# and
#   2 rules on record are gone. I'm not sure what removed them.
#   1. Blocks unless you ask: `<rule>`, on record in the saved copy of your
#      rules (`<snapshot>` on main)
#   2. Allows: `<rule>`, on record in line 12 of `<ledger file>`
#   What would you like to do?
#   • Show me them  • Put them back  • Leave them out
# and
#   I found 1 rule that expired but is still live.
#   1. Allows: `<rule>`, line 7 of `<ledger file>` gave it until
#      2026-11-02 03:00 +07
#   What would you like to do?
#   • Show me  • Keep it  • Remove it
# Each tags the owner and stays open until the owner answers it in its
# thread. Without an owner set (GP_GUARD_OWNER) the messages carry no
# options, and no reply is read.
#
# The owner's answer: each run reads the replies in the thread of each open
# message through the Buzz adapter's one-shot thread read (`launch.mjs
# thread`, which checks every signature), and acts only on a reply whose
# author is the owner's key (checked again here, on the signed event's
# pubkey, never on its text) and whose text is one of the options, with an
# optional leading mention or bullet, "it" or "them", any case. The first
# such answer counts; a later one, or anything else, is ignored. "Show me
# them" posts the rules in the thread (and may be asked again). The others:
#   Keep them      the settings stay; each rule gets an "add" ledger line
#                  (an expired one too: it then has no time limit).
#   Remove them    each rule is taken out of the live settings; "remove" line.
#   Put them back  each rule goes back into its list (a missing list is made
#                  as ["$defaults", <rule>], as the sign script does); "add".
#   Leave them out the settings stay; each rule gets a "remove" ledger line.
# Each acts only on that message's rules that are still in its kind now (so
# an answer applied twice changes nothing more), then the guard says what it
# did in the thread. A rule with an invisible or control character is never
# signed into the ledger by a reply: Keep and Leave out leave it as it is
# (it comes up again); Remove and Put back change the settings without a
# ledger line for it. "$defaults" gets no ledger line either: Put back puts
# it first in its list; Leave out forgets that the list had it, but while
# the snapshot holds it, it comes up again. No answer leaves everything as
# it is, however long.
# When more rules of the same kind turn up while a message is open, a new
# message asks about all of them, and the old thread is no longer read.
# When an open message's rules are all fixed some other way, it is closed
# quietly.
#
# The guard also sends one message when the check itself cannot run (the
# settings file, the snapshot or the ledger unreadable or not what it should
# be), and one more when it runs again.
#
# With BUZZ_TASK_OPEN_EMOJI set, each question and each message that the
# check can't run is a task root: once it is sent and on record, the guard
# puts the open state on it through the adapter's one-shot task (`launch.mjs
# task set`). Best effort: a failure is logged, and the message still counts
# as sent. The all-clear and the guard's replies in a thread get no state.
#
# The installer (install/lib/service.sh, step 14) copies this script and the
# Buzz adapter into a root-owned folder,
# /usr/local/lib/ghost-protocol/<name>/automode-guard/, so the instance's user
# can't change what the timer runs. From a checkout, it finds the adapter in
# the repo.
#
# What it reads, keeps, writes and sends:
#   - The snapshot: read with /usr/bin/git from the instance repo's .git
#     (bound read-only into the unit) as committed on the branch main
#     (refs/heads/main, so a tag named main can't stand in for it), never from
#     the working tree an agent edits, and never fetched from a remote. The
#     tree entry must be a plain file: a committed symlink, or a path through
#     a committed folder symlink, is a broken check.
#   - The settings file: read whole, but only the three autoMode lists are
#     used; nothing else in it (its env block may hold live keys) reaches a
#     message, the log or the state. It is written only to apply the owner's
#     Remove or Put back: in place (the unit binds this one file, so a rename
#     can't replace it), only after the file reads back unchanged since it
#     was read and is still the file in place (a link count above 0), then
#     read back once more. A shorter text is padded with spaces to the old
#     size before the file is cut, so it is valid JSON at every step of a
#     write that runs to the end. A write that fails is undone from the copy
#     in memory. Text UTF-8 can't hold elsewhere in the file (a lone
#     surrogate from a \ud800 escape) is written as escapes, the same JSON;
#     in a rule, it is a broken check. Before each write, the three lists before and after go to
#     $STATE_DIRECTORY/backups/ (0600, the newest 20 kept), never the rest.
#   - The ledger (GP_GUARD_LEDGER, default $STATE_DIRECTORY/ledger.md): the
#     signing record, one line per signed change, as an instance's sign
#     script writes it:
#       - <add|remove> | autoMode.<list> | expires <never|YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM +HH> | <source> | entry sha256 <16 hex> | <rule>
#     The latest line for a rule (its list and exact text) decides; a line
#     whose entry sha256 does not match its rule is ignored. The guard
#     appends one line per rule it acts on, with "reply <event id>" as the
#     source, opened for appending (never renamed). Its expiry is "never",
#     but a rule put back keeps the expiry of the line that signed it.
#     A line is a record, not a proof: the guard does not check who wrote
#     it, and the instance's user can (see read_ledger).
#     A ledger the manifest names must be there: a missing one is a broken
#     check (the installer makes it). Without a named ledger, the guard keeps
#     its own, so a rule signed only by an instance's sign script elsewhere is
#     asked about as a rule the owner didn't make.
#   - State: $STATE_DIRECTORY/state.json: whether the check was last broken
#     (a sha256 of the reason), each open message's event id, its rules as
#     sha256 digests, and the replies already handled, and the names of the
#     lists the guard has seen "$defaults" in. Never a rule. It is
#     written (a temp file, then a rename) after each step that changes it.
#   - Credentials: systemd hands them over in $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY (the
#     unit's drop-in names them). The guard reads them only for the adapter,
#     and gives them to the adapter's process alone, each as an environment
#     variable, by the same rule as install/service/start: the name
#     upper-cased, with - and . as _ (api-token becomes API_TOKEN), and names
#     such as PATH, LD_*, NODE_* or BUZZ_* refused. One BUZZ_ name is let
#     through: BUZZ_AUTH_TAG, the owner's NIP-OA attestation, which the
#     adapter adds to its relay login.
#   - The adapter runs with the unit's environment plus those variables, and
#     its own empty XDG config, cache, data and state folders in the unit's
#     private /tmp, so a key command that keeps a config folder works under
#     the read-only home.
#   - The log (journalctl) gets counts, event ids and outcomes, never a rule
#     and never a reply's text.
#
# Settings (the unit sets them): GP_GUARD_NAME, GP_GUARD_REPO (the instance
# repo), GP_GUARD_SNAPSHOT (the snapshot's path in it), GP_GUARD_SETTINGS
# (default ~/.claude/settings.json), GP_GUARD_LEDGER (an absolute path, or
# empty for the state folder's), GP_GUARD_OWNER (the owner's public key, 64
# hex, or empty), GP_GUARD_NODE (node 22 or later), GP_GUARD_CHANNEL (the
# Buzz channel), GP_GUARD_MENTIONS (public keys, space-separated; the owner
# is always one), GP_GUARD_SEND_PATH (the adapter's PATH, for the key
# command; default /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin), and the adapter's own
# BUZZ_RELAY_URL and BUZZ_KEY_COMMAND, and BUZZ_TASK_OPEN_EMOJI with its
# BUZZ_TASK_OPEN_EMOJI_URL (empty: no state). The guard itself runs /bin/bash,
# /usr/bin/python3 and /usr/bin/git, with PATH=/usr/bin:/bin; only the
# adapter's process gets GP_GUARD_SEND_PATH.
#
# Size: a message is kept under 30,000 bytes of UTF-8 (the send takes 32 KB).
# "Show me them" splits the rules over as many messages as that needs, and
# cuts a rule too long for one.
#
# Usage:
#   guard             one run (what the unit runs)
#   guard --dry-run   what a run would do now, and the messages it would
#                     send, printed here: no credential read, no reply read,
#                     nothing sent, nothing written. Without $STATE_DIRECTORY
#                     it reads the state from
#                     /var/lib/ghost-protocol/<name>-automode-guard/.
#
# Exit: 0 = the check ran and nothing failed; 1 = the check is broken, or a
#       message, a reply read or an answer did not go through (tried again
#       next run); 2 = usage or setup. Anything else that goes wrong is a
#       broken check too, reported once: the guard never ends a run silently
#       on an error it did not plan for (the log names its type only).
#
# Tests only (GP_TEST=1): GP_GUARD_SEND (a command that stands in for
# `node launch.mjs`: it gets `send …`, `thread …` or `task …`), GP_GUARD_RETRY_SECS
# (default 5), GP_GUARD_STATE (the dry run's state folder), GP_GUARD_NOW (the
# time, for expiry dates), GP_GUARD_FAULT (one fault point: classify raises;
# settings-changed and settings-replaced change the settings file between its
# read and its write; write-fails fails the write halfway; ledger-fails fails
# the ledger lines; reload-fails fails the read after an answer). Without
# GP_TEST=1 they are ignored.
#
# Limits: this is a drift detector, not a security boundary against a
# malicious agent. It catches what changes the rules by accident or in
# passing: a tool that rewrites the settings file, a sync, a hand edit, a
# save from a stale copy. A process running as the instance's user can change
# the settings file, the snapshot (the local branch main too), the ledger,
# this guard's state and the key command's files; on a host where that user
# has passwordless sudo, it can change the guard itself. The snapshot and the
# ledger decide what stays quiet; what acts is only a reply the owner's key
# signed. When the agent's user no longer has passwordless sudo, this is to
# be looked at again, with silence resting on owner-signed records alone.
# If Claude Code replaces the settings file (a rename) after the unit
# started, the guard's write is refused (link count 0); one that lands in the
# instant between its last check and its write goes to the replaced file and
# is lost, and the next run asks again. A reply is acted on at the next run,
# up to 15 minutes later. The guard can't report a send that never works (the
# relay or the key command down for good): it logs each failure and tries
# again every run.
set -euo pipefail

DRY=0
case "${1:-}" in
    "") ;;
    --dry-run) DRY=1 ;;
    -h|--help) sed -n '2,/^set -euo/p' "$0" | sed -e '$d' -e 's/^# \{0,1\}//'; exit 0 ;;
    *) echo "usage: guard [--dry-run]" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
[ $# -le 1 ] || { echo "usage: guard [--dry-run]" >&2; exit 2; }

HERE="$(CDPATH='' cd -- "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd -P)"
if [ -f "$HERE/buzz/src/launch.mjs" ]; then
    BUZZ_DIR="$HERE/buzz"                   # the installed copy
else
    BUZZ_DIR="$HERE/../../adapters/buzz"    # a checkout
fi
PYTHON=/usr/bin/python3
[ -x "$PYTHON" ] || { echo "guard: $PYTHON is needed" >&2; exit 2; }
GP_GUARD_DRY="$DRY" GP_GUARD_BUZZ="$BUZZ_DIR" exec "$PYTHON" -I - <<'PY'
import hashlib, json, os, re, shutil, stat, subprocess, sys, tempfile, time, unicodedata
from datetime import datetime

E = os.environ
TEST = E.get("GP_TEST") == "1"
DRY = E["GP_GUARD_DRY"] == "1"
BUZZ = E["GP_GUARD_BUZZ"]
NAME = E.get("GP_GUARD_NAME", "")
REPO = E.get("GP_GUARD_REPO", "")
SNAPSHOT = E.get("GP_GUARD_SNAPSHOT", "")   # a path in REPO, read as committed on main
GIT = "/usr/bin/git"
SEND_PATH = E.get("GP_GUARD_SEND_PATH") or "/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin"
SETTINGS = E.get("GP_GUARD_SETTINGS") or os.path.join(E.get("HOME", "/nonexistent"), ".claude", "settings.json")
LEDGER_SET = E.get("GP_GUARD_LEDGER", "")
OWNER = E.get("GP_GUARD_OWNER", "")
NODE = E.get("GP_GUARD_NODE", "")
CHANNEL = E.get("GP_GUARD_CHANNEL", "")
MENTIONS = E.get("GP_GUARD_MENTIONS", "").split()
ADAPTER_STUB = E.get("GP_GUARD_SEND", "") if TEST else ""
# The reaction that marks an alert open (a task root), from the unit (the
# manifest's buzz_task_open_emoji); empty: alerts get no state.
TASK_EMOJI = E.get("BUZZ_TASK_OPEN_EMOJI", "")
RETRY_SECS = float(E.get("GP_GUARD_RETRY_SECS", "5")) if TEST else 5.0
NOW = int(E["GP_GUARD_NOW"]) if TEST and re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]{1,12}", E.get("GP_GUARD_NOW", "")) else int(time.time())
FAULT = E.get("GP_GUARD_FAULT", "") if TEST else ""   # a test's fault point (see the head)
UNIT = f"{NAME}-automode-guard"
KEYS = ("allow", "soft_deny", "hard_deny")
DEFAULTS = "$defaults"
KINDS = ("unmade", "gone", "expired")
MAX_BYTES = 30000   # a message's UTF-8 size; the send takes 32 KB
ITEM_BYTES = 4000   # one rule's line in a question; Show me them shows it whole
BACKUPS_KEPT = 20
HEX64 = re.compile(r"^[0-9a-f]{64}$")
# Names a credential may not take in the adapter's environment: start's list,
# plus what would steer node, python or the adapter.
RESERVED = re.compile(r"^(PATH|HOME|USER|LOGNAME|SHELL|IFS|ENV|BASH_ENV|PS4|TERM|TMUX|TMPDIR|"
                      r"CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY|STATE_DIRECTORY|LD_.*|DYLD_.*|BASH_FUNC_.*|GP_.*|"
                      r"NODE_.*|NPM_.*|PYTHON.*|BUZZ_.*|XDG_.*)$")
# The one reserved name a credential may take: the owner's attestation, which
# the adapter puts in its relay login (a buzz-auth-tag credential, as for start).
PASSED = frozenset({"BUZZ_AUTH_TAG"})


def log(msg):
    print("guard: " + msg, flush=True)


def die(msg):
    print("guard: ERROR: " + msg, file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
    sys.exit(2)


if not re.fullmatch(r"[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*", NAME):
    die("GP_GUARD_NAME is not an instance name (the unit sets it)")
PLAIN = r"/[A-Za-z0-9._+-]+(/[A-Za-z0-9._+-]+)*"
if not re.fullmatch(PLAIN, REPO):
    die("GP_GUARD_REPO is not a plain path (the unit sets it)")
if (not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+(/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)*", SNAPSHOT)
        or any(part in (".", "..") for part in SNAPSHOT.split("/"))):
    die("GP_GUARD_SNAPSHOT is not a path in the instance repo (the unit sets it)")
if not re.fullmatch(f"{PLAIN}(:{PLAIN})*", SEND_PATH):
    die("GP_GUARD_SEND_PATH is not a list of plain paths (the unit sets it)")
if LEDGER_SET and (not re.fullmatch(PLAIN, LEDGER_SET) or any(p in (".", "..") for p in LEDGER_SET.split("/"))):
    die("GP_GUARD_LEDGER is not a plain absolute path (the unit sets it)")
if OWNER and not HEX64.match(OWNER):
    die("GP_GUARD_OWNER is not a public key of 64 lower-case hex characters (the unit sets it)")
if OWNER and OWNER not in MENTIONS:
    MENTIONS.append(OWNER)
if E.get("STATE_DIRECTORY"):
    STATE_DIR = E["STATE_DIRECTORY"].split(":")[0]
elif DRY:
    STATE_DIR = (E.get("GP_GUARD_STATE") if TEST else "") or f"/var/lib/ghost-protocol/{UNIT}"
else:
    die("no $STATE_DIRECTORY: run it through its unit, or with --dry-run")
STATE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "state.json")
OLD_STATE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "last-alert")   # the state file before state.json
LEDGER = LEDGER_SET or os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "ledger.md")
if os.path.realpath(LEDGER) == os.path.realpath(SETTINGS):
    die("GP_GUARD_LEDGER is the settings file")


class ApplyError(Exception):
    """Why an answer could not be applied, in fixed words (never file content)."""


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- text

def escape_invisible(text):
    out = []
    for c in text:
        if unicodedata.category(c) in ("Cc", "Cf", "Co", "Cs", "Cn", "Zl", "Zp"):
            o = ord(c)
            out.append(f"\\u{o:04x}" if o <= 0xFFFF else f"\\U{o:08x}")
        else:
            out.append(c)
    return "".join(out)


def hidden(rule):
    """True when a rule holds a character a ledger line can't carry as seen."""
    return any(unicodedata.category(c) in ("Cc", "Cf", "Zl", "Zp") for c in rule)


def cut(s, n):
    return s if len(s) <= n else s[: n - 1] + "…"


def code(s):
    """s as a Markdown code span, its fence longer than any backtick run in it."""
    s = escape_invisible(s)
    run = max((len(m) for m in re.findall(r"`+", s)), default=0)
    fence = "`" * (run + 1)
    pad = " " if s.startswith("`") or s.endswith("`") else ""
    return f"{fence}{pad}{s}{pad}{fence}"


def sha(s):
    return hashlib.sha256(s.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


def rule_id(k, rule):
    return sha(f"{k}\0{rule}")


def rules_word(n):
    return "1 rule" if n == 1 else f"{n} rules"


def the_rules(n):
    return "the rule" if n == 1 else f"the {n} rules"


# The owner's words, as approved for the first kind; "them" is "it" for one
# rule. The other two never say who made a rule: the snapshot and the ledger
# that put a rule on record are files the instance's user can write (see
# read_ledger), so a question about them quotes each rule and says where it
# is on record, and claims nothing more.
def ask_text(kind, rules, view):
    """The question about rules ([(list, rule)], in order) of one kind."""
    n = len(rules)
    it = "it" if n == 1 else "them"
    show = "Show me" if n == 1 else "Show me them"
    if kind == "unmade":
        head = f"I found {rules_word(n)} you didn't make. Another agent may have added {it}; I'm not sure."
        if not OWNER:
            return head
        return "\n".join([f"{head} What would you like to do?", f"• {show}", f"• Keep {it}", f"• Remove {it}"])
    if kind == "gone":
        head = f"{rules_word(n)} on record {'is' if n == 1 else 'are'} gone. I'm not sure what removed {it}."
        options = [f"• {show}", f"• Put {it} back", f"• Leave {it} out"]
    else:   # expired
        head = f"I found {rules_word(n)} that expired but {'is' if n == 1 else 'are'} still live."
        options = [f"• {show}", f"• Keep {it}", f"• Remove {it}"]
    tail = ["What would you like to do?"] + options if OWNER else []
    if OWNER and kind == "expired":
        tail = [f"If you keep {it}, {'it' if n == 1 else 'they'} won't have a time limit any more."] + tail
    lines, items = [head], rule_items(rules, note_for(kind, view), ITEM_BYTES)
    budget = MAX_BYTES - 200
    for j, item in enumerate(items):
        if len("\n".join(lines + [item] + tail).encode("utf-8")) > budget - 100:
            lines.append(f"…and {len(items) - j} more" + (": say Show me them to see them all." if OWNER else "."))
            break
        lines.append(item)
    return "\n".join(lines + tail)


BROKEN_TEXT = "I can't check your rules right now, so I won't notice if they change. I'll tell you when I can again."
CLEAR_TEXT = "I can check your rules again."
FAILED_TEXT = "I couldn't change the rules just now. I'll try again in a few minutes."
NOTHING_TEXT = "Nothing to do: those rules changed since I asked."
LABEL = {"allow": "Allows", "soft_deny": "Blocks unless you ask", "hard_deny": "Always blocks"}
DONE = {"keep": "I kept {}.", "remove": "I removed {}.", "put": "I put {} back.", "leave": "I left {} out."}


def note_for(kind, view):
    """Where each rule of a kind is on record, as a function of (list, rule); None for none."""
    ledger_name = code(os.path.basename(LEDGER))

    def gone(k, rule):
        if rule in view["snap"][k]:
            return f"on record in the saved copy of your rules ({code(SNAPSHOT)} on main)"
        if rule == DEFAULTS:
            return "it was in your settings when I looked before"
        return f"on record in line {view['ledger'][(k, rule)][3]} of {ledger_name}"

    def expired(k, rule):
        last = view["ledger"][(k, rule)]
        if last[1] is None:   # not "never" (that is signed): a time limit that can't be read
            return f"line {last[3]} of {ledger_name} gives it a time limit I can't read ({code(last[2])}), so I count it as passed"
        return f"line {last[3]} of {ledger_name} gave it until {last[2]}"

    return {"gone": gone, "expired": expired}.get(kind)


def rule_item(i, k, rule, note=""):
    gloss = " (Claude Code's built-in rules)" if rule == DEFAULTS else ""
    return f"{i}. {LABEL[k]}: {code(rule)}{gloss}" + (f", {note}" if note else "")


def rule_items(rules, note, budget):
    """Each rule as a numbered line of at most budget bytes, its text cut to fit."""
    items = []
    for i, (k, rule) in enumerate(rules, 1):
        n = note(k, rule) if note else ""
        item, width = rule_item(i, k, rule, n), len(rule)
        while len(item.encode("utf-8")) > budget and width > 1:   # never a loop with no end
            width = width // 2
            item = rule_item(i, k, cut(rule, width), n)
        items.append(item)
    return items


def done_text(kind, choice, done, skipped):
    parts = []
    if done:
        what = DONE[choice].format(the_rules(len(done)))
        if kind == "expired" and choice == "keep":
            what = what[:-1] + ", with no time limit now."
        parts.append("Done: " + what)
    hid = [t for t in skipped if t[1] != DEFAULTS]
    if hid:
        parts.append(f"I left {rules_word(len(hid))} as {'it is' if len(hid) == 1 else 'they are'}: "
                     f"{'it has' if len(hid) == 1 else 'they have'} hidden characters, so I'll ask again.")
    if any(r == DEFAULTS for _, r in skipped):
        parts.append("Claude Code's built-in rules are in the saved copy of your rules on main, "
                     "so I'll ask about them again until that copy changes.")
    return " ".join(parts) or NOTHING_TEXT


def show_texts(rules, note=None):
    """The rules, numbered, over as few messages of MAX_BYTES as they fit in."""
    if not rules:
        return [NOTHING_TEXT]
    head = "Here it is:" if len(rules) == 1 else f"Here they are ({len(rules)}):"
    budget = MAX_BYTES - 200
    items = rule_items(rules, note, budget)
    out, cur = [], [head]
    for item in items:
        if len("\n".join(cur + [item]).encode("utf-8")) > budget:
            out.append("\n".join(cur))
            cur = []
        cur.append(item)
    out.append("\n".join(cur))
    return out


# The options. A reply counts when, after a leading mention or bullet is taken
# off and the case is folded, it is exactly one of these.
OPTIONS = {
    "show": {"show", "show me", "show me them", "show me it", "show them", "show it",
             "show them to me", "show it to me"},
    "keep": {"keep", "keep them", "keep it"},
    "remove": {"remove", "remove them", "remove it"},
    "put": {"put them back", "put it back", "put back"},
    "leave": {"leave them out", "leave it out", "leave out"},
}
VALID = {"unmade": {"show", "keep", "remove"}, "gone": {"show", "put", "leave"},
         "expired": {"show", "keep", "remove"}}


def parse_reply(content):
    t = content.replace("’", "'").strip().lower()
    t = re.sub(r"^(?:(?:@\S+|nostr:npub1[0-9a-z]+),?\s+)+", "", t)
    t = t.lstrip("•*-–— ").strip()
    t = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", t).rstrip(".! ").strip()
    for choice, words in OPTIONS.items():
        if t in words:
            return choice
    return None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- reading

def lists_of(obj, where):
    """The allow / soft_deny / hard_deny lists of obj ((lists, None)), a missing key or null as []."""
    if obj is None:
        obj = {}
    if not isinstance(obj, dict):
        return None, f"{where} is not a JSON object"
    out = {}
    for k in KEYS:
        v = obj.get(k)
        if v is None:
            v = []
        if not isinstance(v, list) or not all(isinstance(s, str) for s in v):
            return None, f"{where}.{k} is not a list of strings"
        for i, rule in enumerate(v, 1):
            try:
                rule.encode("utf-8")
            except UnicodeEncodeError:   # a lone surrogate, from a \ud800 escape
                return None, f"{where}.{k} rule {i} is not valid Unicode text"
        out[k] = list(v)
    return out, None


def no_constant(word):
    """NaN, Infinity and -Infinity: Python reads them, JSON (and Claude Code) does not."""
    raise ValueError(word)


def parse_json(raw, what, shown):
    """(object, None) or (None, reason): the reason names the path and a position, never content."""
    try:
        text = raw.decode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        return None, f"{what} is not UTF-8 text: {shown}"
    try:
        return json.loads(text, parse_constant=no_constant), None
    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:
        return None, f"{what} is not valid JSON: {shown} (line {e.lineno}, column {e.colno})"
    except ValueError:
        return None, f"{what} is not valid JSON: {shown} (NaN or Infinity, which JSON does not allow)"
    except RecursionError:
        return None, f"{what} is nested too deeply: {shown}"


def git(*args):
    """git on the instance repo's own objects, reading only: (result, None) or (None, reason)."""
    env = {"PATH": "/usr/bin:/bin", "LANG": "C.UTF-8", "HOME": E.get("HOME", "/nonexistent"),
           "GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM": "1", "GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL": "/dev/null", "GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS": "0",
           # A partial clone must not fetch a missing object from its remote (git 2.44+).
           "GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH": "1"}
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([GIT, "--git-dir", os.path.join(REPO, ".git"), "-c", "core.fsmonitor=false", *args],
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True, timeout=30, env=env, cwd="/")
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None, f"git is missing: {GIT}"
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return None, "git did not finish within 30 seconds"
    except OSError as e:
        return None, f"git could not start ({e.strerror})"
    return r, None


def snapshot_content():
    """The snapshot as committed on main: (bytes, None), else (None, reason)."""
    gd = os.path.join(REPO, ".git")
    if os.path.islink(gd) or not os.path.isdir(gd):
        return None, f"the instance repo {REPO} has no .git folder of its own"
    # The full ref: a tag named main would outrank a bare "main".
    r, why = git("ls-tree", "refs/heads/main", "--", SNAPSHOT)
    if r is None:
        return None, why
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None, f"could not read branch main in {REPO} (git exited {r.returncode})"
    line = r.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").rstrip("\n")
    m = re.fullmatch(r"(?:100644|100755) blob ([0-9a-f]{40}|[0-9a-f]{64})\t" + re.escape(SNAPSHOT), line)
    if not m:
        if not line:
            return None, (f"the snapshot {SNAPSHOT} is not on main in {REPO} "
                          "(a path through a symlinked folder is not followed)")
        if line.startswith("120000 "):
            return None, f"the snapshot {SNAPSHOT} is a symlink on main in {REPO}; it must be a plain file"
        return None, f"the snapshot {SNAPSHOT} is not a plain file on main in {REPO}"
    r, why = git("cat-file", "blob", m.group(1))
    if r is None:
        return None, why
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None, f"could not read the snapshot from main in {REPO} (git exited {r.returncode})"
    return r.stdout, None


def read_settings():
    """The settings file's three lists: (lists, None) or (None, reason)."""
    try:
        with open(SETTINGS, "rb") as f:
            raw = f.read()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None, f"settings file not found: {SETTINGS}"
    except OSError as e:
        return None, f"cannot read the settings file {SETTINGS} ({e.strerror})"
    data, why = parse_json(raw, "settings file", SETTINGS)
    if why:
        return None, why
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return None, f"settings file is not a JSON object: {SETTINGS}"
    return lists_of(data.get("autoMode"), "autoMode")


# A ledger line, as an instance's sign script writes it (and this guard does).
LEDGER_LINE = re.compile(
    r"^- (add|remove) \| autoMode\.(allow|soft_deny|hard_deny) \| expires "
    r"(never|[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2} [+-][0-9]{2}) \| ([^|\n]+?) "
    r"\| entry sha256 ([0-9a-f]{16}) \| (.+)$")


def expiry(exp):
    """An expiry as a unix time; None for never, and None for a date that does
    not parse: signed() tells the two apart by the text, so a date that does
    not parse is never taken as never."""
    if exp == "never":
        return None
    try:
        return int(datetime.strptime(exp + "00", "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M %z").timestamp())
    except ValueError:
        return None


def signed(last):
    """True when a rule's latest ledger line is an add still in force: one that
    never expires, or whose expiry is still to come. An expired add is not,
    and nor is one whose expiry does not parse (invalid, never "never")."""
    return bool(last) and last[0] == "add" and (last[2] == "never" or (last[1] is not None and last[1] > NOW))


def read_ledger():
    """{(list, rule): (latest action, expiry or None, expiry as written, line number)}:
    (ledger, None), or (None, reason). The guard's own ledger (in its state
    folder) that is not there yet is empty; a ledger the manifest names must
    be there (the installer makes it), or every rule signed in it would look
    unsigned.

    A ledger line is a record, not a proof of who made a rule. The installer
    makes the ledger 0600, owned by the instance's user, the user the agent
    runs as, and nothing here checks the file's owner or mode (no check of
    them could tell a line the agent wrote from one the owner's sign script
    wrote). The same user can commit to the snapshot's branch main. So a
    question never says the owner made a rule: it quotes the rule and says
    where it is on record (ask_text)."""
    try:
        with open(LEDGER, "rb") as f:
            raw = f.read()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        if LEDGER_SET and not DRY:
            return None, f"the ledger {LEDGER} is not there (rerun the installer to make it)"
        if LEDGER_SET:
            log(f"(dry run) the ledger {LEDGER} is not there yet (the installer makes it); read as empty")
        return {}, None
    except OSError as e:
        return None, f"cannot read the ledger {LEDGER} ({e.strerror})"
    last, bad = {}, 0
    for n, line in enumerate(raw.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\n"), 1):
        m = LEDGER_LINE.match(line)
        if not m:
            continue
        action, k, exp, _source, entry, rule = m.groups()
        if sha(rule)[:16] != entry:
            bad += 1
            continue
        last[(k, rule)] = (action, expiry(exp), exp, n)
    if bad:
        log(f"the ledger has {bad} line(s) whose entry sha256 does not match their rule; ignored")
    return last, None


def classify(live, snap, ledger, seen):
    """The rules to ask about: {"unmade": {digest: (list, rule)}, "gone": {...},
    "expired": {...}}. seen: the lists the guard has seen "$defaults" in, live."""
    if FAULT == "classify":
        raise RuntimeError("a test's fault")
    out = {kind: {} for kind in KINDS}
    for k in KEYS:
        lv, sv = set(live[k]), set(snap[k])
        for rule in live[k]:
            if rule == DEFAULTS or rule in sv:
                continue
            last = ledger.get((k, rule))
            if signed(last):
                continue
            # A grant that expired, or whose expiry does not parse, is live
            # but no longer signed.
            out["expired" if last and last[0] == "add" else "unmade"].setdefault(rule_id(k, rule), (k, rule))
        # "$defaults" (Claude Code's own default rules) gone from a list that
        # had it, in the snapshot or in the live settings as the guard saw
        # them before: always asked about. No ledger line counts for it either
        # way, so a line can't sign it away; it is never asked about as added.
        if DEFAULTS not in lv and (DEFAULTS in sv or k in seen):
            out["gone"].setdefault(rule_id(k, DEFAULTS), (k, DEFAULTS))
        owned = list(snap[k]) + [r for (kk, r), last in ledger.items() if kk == k and signed(last)]
        for rule in owned:
            if rule == DEFAULTS or rule in lv:
                continue
            last = ledger.get((k, rule))
            if last and last[0] == "remove":   # taken out, on record
                continue
            if last and last[0] == "add" and last[1] is not None and last[1] <= NOW:   # an expired grant
                continue
            out["gone"].setdefault(rule_id(k, rule), (k, rule))
    return out


def load(seen):
    """Everything a run needs: (view, None), or (None, reason) when the check can't run."""
    content, why = snapshot_content()
    if content is None:
        return None, why
    obj, why = parse_json(content, "snapshot", f"main:{SNAPSHOT}")
    if why:
        return None, why
    snap, why = lists_of(obj, "snapshot")
    if why:
        return None, why
    live, why = read_settings()
    if why:
        return None, why
    ledger, why = read_ledger()
    if why:
        return None, why
    return {"live": live, "snap": snap, "ledger": ledger, "classes": classify(live, snap, ledger, seen)}, None


def check(seen):
    view, reason = load(seen)
    if view is None:
        # Once more after a pause: a run that caught a file half written is
        # not a broken check.
        time.sleep(RETRY_SECS)
        view, reason = load(seen)
    return view, reason


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- state

def fresh_state():
    return {"version": 2, "check": "ok", "alerts": {}, "defaults": []}


def valid_alert(a):
    return (isinstance(a, dict) and set(a) <= {"id", "rules", "handled", "posted", "failed"}
            and isinstance(a.get("id"), str) and HEX64.match(a["id"])
            and isinstance(a.get("rules"), list) and 0 < len(a["rules"]) <= 100000
            and all(isinstance(d, str) and HEX64.match(d) for d in a["rules"])
            and isinstance(a.get("handled"), list) and len(a["handled"]) <= 1000
            and all(isinstance(d, str) and HEX64.match(d) for d in a["handled"])
            and isinstance(a.get("posted"), int) and not isinstance(a["posted"], bool)
            and isinstance(a.get("failed", False), bool))


def read_state():
    try:
        with open(STATE, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            raw = f.read()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        st = fresh_state()
        # The first run after an update: a broken check stays reported.
        try:
            with open(OLD_STATE, encoding="utf-8") as f:
                old = f.read().strip()
            if re.fullmatch(r"broken:[0-9a-f]{64}", old):
                st["check"] = old
        except OSError:
            pass
        return st
    except OSError as e:
        if DRY:
            log(f"(dry run) cannot read {STATE} ({e.strerror}); taking it as empty")
            return fresh_state()
        raise
    try:
        obj = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        obj = None
    # "defaults" (the lists the guard has seen "$defaults" in, live) came
    # after the first state.json, so a state without it is read as none.
    if (isinstance(obj, dict) and obj.get("version") == 2
            and {"version", "check", "alerts"} <= set(obj) <= {"version", "check", "alerts", "defaults"}
            and isinstance(obj["check"], str) and re.fullmatch(r"ok|broken:[0-9a-f]{64}", obj["check"])
            and isinstance(obj["alerts"], dict) and set(obj["alerts"]) <= set(KINDS)
            and all(valid_alert(a) for a in obj["alerts"].values())
            and isinstance(obj.get("defaults", []), list)
            and all(isinstance(d, str) and d in KEYS for d in obj.get("defaults", []))):
        obj["defaults"] = sorted(set(obj.get("defaults", [])))
        return obj
    log("the state file holds nothing usable; starting from nothing on record")
    return fresh_state()


def write_state(st):
    if DRY:
        return
    fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=STATE_DIR, prefix=".state.")
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            json.dump(st, f, sort_keys=True)
            f.write("\n")
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        os.replace(tmp, STATE)
    except BaseException:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the adapter

def credentials():
    """Each credential as (variable, value), by start's naming rule; none on any doubt."""
    out, d = [], E.get("CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY", "")
    if not d or not os.path.isdir(d):
        return out
    for name in sorted(os.listdir(d)):
        path = os.path.join(d, name)
        if not os.path.isfile(path):
            continue
        var = name.upper().replace("-", "_").replace(".", "_")
        if not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*", var):
            log("skipped a credential: its name is not a variable name")
            continue
        if RESERVED.match(var) and var not in PASSED:
            log(f"skipped credential {var}: that name is reserved")
            continue
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            out.append((var, f.read().rstrip("\n")))
    return out


def adapter(args, text=None, timeout=150):
    """Runs `node launch.mjs <args>`: (exit code, stdout), -1 when it could not run.
    Logs the outcome in fixed words, never the text, a reply or a credential."""
    if ADAPTER_STUB:
        argv = [ADAPTER_STUB]
    else:
        launch = os.path.join(BUZZ, "src", "launch.mjs")
        if not NODE or not os.path.isfile(NODE):
            log(f"the adapter can't run: node is missing ({NODE or 'GP_GUARD_NODE is not set'}); rerun the installer")
            return -1, ""
        if not os.path.isfile(launch):
            log(f"the adapter can't run: the Buzz adapter's launcher is missing: {launch}")
            return -1, ""
        argv = [NODE, launch]
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9-]{1,64}", CHANNEL):
        log("the adapter can't run: GP_GUARD_CHANNEL is not a channel id")
        return -1, ""
    argv += args
    scratch = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="guard-adapter.")   # 0700, in the unit's private /tmp
    env = dict(E)
    for k in ("GP_GUARD_DRY", "GP_GUARD_BUZZ"):
        env.pop(k, None)
    # The guard's own PATH is /usr/bin:/bin; the adapter (its key command) gets the unit's wider one.
    env["PATH"] = SEND_PATH
    for sub in ("config", "cache", "data", "state"):
        p = os.path.join(scratch, sub)
        os.mkdir(p, 0o700)
        env[f"XDG_{sub.upper()}_HOME"] = p
    try:
        for var, value in credentials():
            env[var] = value
        r = subprocess.run(argv, input=text if text is not None else "", capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=timeout, env=env, cwd="/")
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        log(f"the adapter ({args[0]}) did not finish within {timeout} seconds")
        return -1, ""
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"the adapter ({args[0]}) could not start ({e.strerror})")
        return -1, ""
    finally:
        env.clear()
        shutil.rmtree(scratch, ignore_errors=True)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        # The adapter's own lines are fixed words or the relay's reason, never the key or the text.
        last = next((ln for ln in reversed((r.stderr or "").splitlines()) if ln.strip()), "")
        log(f"the adapter ({args[0]}) exited {r.returncode}" + (f" ({escape_invisible(cut(last, 200))})" if last else ""))
    return r.returncode, r.stdout or ""


def send(text, reply=None, mention=True):
    """The event id when the relay took the message, else None. A send that
    prints no event id counts as not sent: a message whose thread can't be
    read would be a dead end, so it is sent again next run."""
    if DRY:
        log(f"(dry run) would send to Buzz channel {CHANNEL}" + (f", in the thread of {reply}" if reply else "") + ":")
        print(text, flush=True)
        return "0" * 64
    args = ["send", "--channel", CHANNEL]
    if mention:
        for m in MENTIONS:
            args += ["--mention", m]
    if reply:
        args += ["--reply", reply]
    rc, out = adapter(args, text)
    if rc != 0:
        log("message NOT sent")
        return None
    m = re.search(r"^sent ([0-9a-f]{64})$", out, re.M)
    if not m:
        log("message sent, but no event id came back; it counts as NOT sent")
        return None
    log(f"message sent (event {m.group(1)})")
    return m.group(1)


def mark_open(event_id):
    """Best effort, once an alert went out and its state is written: the open
    state on it, so the alert is a task root (the Buzz adapter's one-shot
    task, src/task.mjs). Only with BUZZ_TASK_OPEN_EMOJI set, and never on a
    dry run. It never raises, and can't change whether the alert counts as
    sent (that is already written): a failure is logged, never retried."""
    if not TASK_EMOJI or DRY:
        return
    if not event_id:
        log("alert NOT marked open: the send printed no event id")
        return
    try:
        rc, _ = adapter(["task", "set", "--channel", CHANNEL, "--id", event_id, "--state", "open"], timeout=60)
    except Exception as e:   # anything: the alert went out, and must not be sent again
        log(f"alert NOT marked open: {type(e).__name__}")
        return
    if rc == 0:
        log("alert marked open (a task root)")
    else:
        log("alert NOT marked open: " + (f"the task tool exited {rc}" if rc != -1 else "the task tool did not run"))


def read_replies(alerts):
    """The replies in the open messages' threads: {root: [reply, ...]}, or None when the read failed."""
    roots = sorted({a["id"] for a in alerts.values()})
    if not roots:
        return {}
    since = max(0, min(a["posted"] for a in alerts.values()) - 600)
    args = ["thread", "--channel", CHANNEL, "--author", OWNER, "--since", str(since)]
    for root in roots:
        args += ["--root", root]
    rc, out = adapter(args, timeout=90)
    if rc != 0:
        log("the replies were NOT read; trying again next run")
        return None
    by_root = {}
    for line in out.splitlines():
        try:
            r = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if (isinstance(r, dict) and isinstance(r.get("id"), str) and HEX64.match(r["id"])
                and isinstance(r.get("pubkey"), str) and isinstance(r.get("root"), str) and r["root"] in roots
                and isinstance(r.get("created_at"), int) and isinstance(r.get("content"), str)):
            by_root.setdefault(r["root"], []).append(r)
    return by_root


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- applying an answer

def read_all(fd):
    os.lseek(fd, 0, os.SEEK_SET)
    chunks = []
    while True:
        b = os.read(fd, 1 << 16)
        if not b:
            return b"".join(chunks)
        chunks.append(b)


def write_all(fd, data, old_size=0):
    """data from the start of the file, then the file cut to it. A shorter
    text is padded with spaces to the old size first (JSON allows trailing
    space), so between the write and the cut the file is still valid JSON."""
    if len(data) < old_size:
        data_padded = data + b" " * (old_size - len(data))
    else:
        data_padded = data
    os.lseek(fd, 0, os.SEEK_SET)
    view = memoryview(data_padded)
    while view:
        n = os.write(fd, view)
        view = view[n:]
    os.ftruncate(fd, len(data))
    os.fsync(fd)


def backup(before, after, reply):
    """The three lists before and after, never the rest of the file; the newest BACKUPS_KEPT kept."""
    d = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "backups")
    os.makedirs(d, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    name = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ", time.gmtime()) + f"-{reply[:16]}.json"
    fd = os.open(os.path.join(d, name), os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        json.dump({"reply": reply, "before": before, "after": after}, f, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)
        f.write("\n")
        f.flush()
        os.fsync(f.fileno())
    for old in sorted(n for n in os.listdir(d) if n.endswith(".json"))[:-BACKUPS_KEPT]:
        try:
            os.unlink(os.path.join(d, old))
        except OSError:
            pass


def fault_before_write(fd):
    """A test's fault point: the settings file changes after the guard read it."""
    if FAULT == "settings-changed":
        os.pwrite(fd, b" ", os.fstat(fd).st_size)
    elif FAULT == "settings-replaced":
        tmp = SETTINGS + ".replaced"
        with open(tmp, "wb") as f:
            f.write(read_all(fd))
        os.replace(tmp, SETTINGS)


def change_settings(change, reply):
    """Applies change(autoMode) to the settings file in place (see the head of this file)."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(SETTINGS, os.O_RDWR | getattr(os, "O_NOFOLLOW", 0))
    except OSError as e:
        raise ApplyError(f"could not open the settings file to write ({e.strerror})")
    try:
        st = os.fstat(fd)
        if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            raise ApplyError("the settings file is not a plain file")
        if st.st_nlink == 0:
            raise ApplyError("the settings file was replaced while the guard ran")
        raw = read_all(fd)
        data, why = parse_json(raw, "settings file", SETTINGS)
        if why:
            raise ApplyError(why)
        if not isinstance(data, dict):
            raise ApplyError("the settings file is not a JSON object")
        if data.get("autoMode") is None:
            data["autoMode"] = {}
        am = data["autoMode"]
        before, why = lists_of(am, "autoMode")
        if why:
            raise ApplyError(why)
        change(am)
        after, why = lists_of(am, "autoMode")
        if why:
            raise ApplyError(why)
        try:
            try:
                new = (json.dumps(data, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False) + "\n").encode("utf-8")
            except UnicodeEncodeError:
                # Text UTF-8 can't hold elsewhere in the file (a lone surrogate
                # from a \ud800 escape): written as escapes, the same JSON.
                new = (json.dumps(data, indent=2, ensure_ascii=True, allow_nan=False) + "\n").encode("ascii")
        except ValueError:
            # A number too large for a float (1e400) reads as infinity, and
            # would be written back as Infinity, which JSON does not allow.
            raise ApplyError("the settings file holds a number the guard can't write back exactly; nothing was written")
        fault_before_write(fd)
        if read_all(fd) != raw or os.fstat(fd).st_nlink == 0:
            raise ApplyError("the settings file changed while the guard ran")
        try:
            backup(before, after, reply)
        except OSError as e:
            raise ApplyError(f"could not keep the backup in the state folder ({e.strerror}); nothing was written")
        try:
            if FAULT == "write-fails":
                os.lseek(fd, 0, os.SEEK_SET)
                os.write(fd, b"#" * (len(new) // 2))   # a write that stops halfway, garbled
                raise OSError(5, "a test's fault")
            write_all(fd, new, len(raw))
            if read_all(fd) != new:
                raise OSError(0, "it did not read back as written")
        except OSError as e:
            try:
                write_all(fd, raw)
            except OSError:
                raise ApplyError("the settings write failed, and putting the file back failed too")
            raise ApplyError(f"the settings write failed ({e.strerror}); the file was put back")
        if os.fstat(fd).st_nlink == 0:
            raise ApplyError("the settings file was replaced while the guard wrote it")
    except OSError as e:
        raise ApplyError(f"could not read or write the settings file ({e.strerror})")
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def ledger_append(rules, action, reply):
    """One ledger line per (list, rule, expiry), appended (never renamed: the unit may bind this one file)."""
    if FAULT == "ledger-fails":
        raise ApplyError("a test's fault")
    lines = "".join(f"- {action} | autoMode.{k} | expires {exp} | reply {reply} | entry sha256 {sha(r)[:16]} | {r}\n"
                    for k, r, exp in rules)
    try:
        fd = os.open(LEDGER, os.O_RDWR | os.O_APPEND | os.O_CREAT | getattr(os, "O_NOFOLLOW", 0), 0o600)
    except OSError as e:
        raise ApplyError(f"could not open the ledger {LEDGER} ({e.strerror})")
    try:
        st = os.fstat(fd)
        if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) or st.st_nlink == 0:
            raise ApplyError("the ledger is not a plain file in place")
        prefix = b""
        if st.st_size > 0 and os.pread(fd, 1, st.st_size - 1) != b"\n":
            prefix = b"\n"
        data = prefix + lines.encode("utf-8")
        view = memoryview(data)
        while view:
            view = view[os.write(fd, view):]
        os.fsync(fd)
    except OSError as e:
        raise ApplyError(f"could not write the ledger {LEDGER} ({e.strerror})")
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def apply(choice, targets, reply, view):
    """Applies one answer to its rules: (done, skipped). Raises ApplyError.
    A rule put back keeps the expiry of the ledger line that signed it, while
    that line is in force. "$defaults" never gets a ledger line: Put back puts
    it first in its list; Leave out leaves it out (the caller forgets the list
    had it), but while the snapshot holds it, it is asked about again."""
    ledger, snap = view["ledger"], view["snap"]

    def expires(k, rule):
        last = ledger.get((k, rule))
        return last[2] if choice == "put" and signed(last) else "never"
    clean = [(k, r, expires(k, r)) for k, r in targets if not hidden(r) and r != DEFAULTS]
    if choice in ("keep", "leave"):
        if clean:
            ledger_append(clean, "add" if choice == "keep" else "remove", reply)
        defaults = [t for t in targets if t[1] == DEFAULTS]
        return ([(k, r) for k, r, _ in clean] + [t for t in defaults if DEFAULTS not in snap[t[0]]],
                [t for t in targets if hidden(t[1])] + [t for t in defaults if DEFAULTS in snap[t[0]]])

    def change(am):
        for k, rule in targets:
            if choice == "remove":
                if isinstance(am.get(k), list):
                    am[k] = [r for r in am[k] if r != rule]
            elif am.get(k) is None:
                am[k] = [DEFAULTS] if rule == DEFAULTS else [DEFAULTS, rule]
            elif rule == DEFAULTS and rule not in am[k]:
                am[k].insert(0, rule)
            elif rule not in am[k]:
                am[k].append(rule)

    if targets:
        change_settings(change, reply)
    if clean:
        try:
            ledger_append(clean, "remove" if choice == "remove" else "add", reply)
        except ApplyError as e:
            log(f"the settings changed, but the ledger lines were NOT written: {e}")
    return targets, []


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- one run

def answer(state, view):
    """Reads and acts on the owner's answers. Returns (view, everything went
    through); view is None when the rules could not be read again after an
    answer, so this run asks nothing from a view that is out of date."""
    alerts = state["alerts"]
    if not OWNER or not alerts:
        return view, True
    if DRY:
        log(f"(dry run) {len(alerts)} open message(s); replies are read on a real run only")
        return view, True
    by_root = read_replies(alerts)
    if by_root is None:
        return view, False
    ok = True
    for kind in KINDS:
        a = alerts.get(kind)
        if not a:
            continue
        for r in sorted(by_root.get(a["id"], []), key=lambda r: (r["created_at"], r["id"])):
            if r["id"] in a["handled"]:
                continue
            # Only the owner's key: the signed event's author, never the text.
            if r["pubkey"] != OWNER:
                log(f"ignored a reply from another key ({r['id']})")
                continue
            choice = parse_reply(r["content"])
            if choice is None or choice not in VALID[kind]:
                a["handled"].append(r["id"])
                write_state(state)
                continue
            current = view["classes"][kind]
            asked = set(a["rules"])
            targets = [rule for d, rule in current.items() if d in asked]   # in the settings' own order
            if choice == "show":
                if not all(send(t, reply=a["id"]) is not None for t in show_texts(targets, note_for(kind, view))):
                    ok = False
                    break
                log(f"showed {len(targets)} rule(s) for {a['id']} (reply {r['id']})")
                a["handled"].append(r["id"])
                write_state(state)
                continue
            try:
                done, skipped = apply(choice, targets, r["id"], view)
            except ApplyError as e:
                log(f"the owner's answer {choice} ({r['id']}) was NOT applied: {e}; trying again next run")
                ok = False
                if not a.get("failed") and send(FAILED_TEXT, reply=a["id"]) is not None:
                    a["failed"] = True
                    write_state(state)
                break
            log(f"applied the owner's answer {choice} ({r['id']}) to {len(done)} rule(s); {len(skipped)} left as they are")
            # The answer is spent: its message is closed in the guard's state.
            # Any open state set on it once it was saved stays; no done state
            # is set yet.
            del alerts[kind]
            if choice == "leave":   # the owner's signed answer: "$defaults" out of those lists is fine now
                state["defaults"] = sorted(set(state["defaults"]) - {k for k, rule in targets if rule == DEFAULTS})
            write_state(state)
            if send(done_text(kind, choice, done, skipped), reply=a["id"], mention=False) is None:
                log("the answer is applied; the note saying so did not go out")
            fresh, why = load(set(state["defaults"])) if FAULT != "reload-fails" else (None, "a test's fault")
            if fresh is None:
                log(f"could not read the rules again after the answer: {escape_invisible(why)}; nothing more is asked this run")
                return None, False
            view = fresh
            break
    return view, ok


def ask(state, view):
    """Posts a message for each kind with rules nobody has asked about. Returns
    (messages posted, everything went through)."""
    posted, ok = 0, True
    for kind in KINDS:
        current = view["classes"][kind]
        a = state["alerts"].get(kind)
        if not current:
            if a:
                # Fixed some other way: closed quietly in the guard's state.
                # Any open state set on it once it was saved stays; no done
                # state is set yet.
                log(f"the {kind} message {a['id']} has nothing left to ask; closed")
                del state["alerts"][kind]
                write_state(state)
            continue
        if a and set(current) <= set(a["rules"]):
            log(f"{kind}: {len(current)} rule(s), already asked ({a['id']})")
            continue
        log(f"{kind}: {len(current)} rule(s) to ask about" + (f" (more than {a['id']} asked)" if a else ""))
        eid = send(ask_text(kind, list(current.values()), view))
        if eid is None:
            ok = False
            continue
        posted += 1
        # A new message replaces the old one, whose thread is no longer read.
        state["alerts"][kind] = {"id": eid, "rules": sorted(current), "handled": [], "posted": NOW}
        write_state(state)
        # Only now, with the question on record: its open state.
        mark_open(eid)   # guard:markopen
    return posted, ok


def writable():
    """The state must be writable before anything goes out: a message whose
    state can't be kept would go out again on every run."""
    if DRY:
        return True
    try:
        fd, probe = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=STATE_DIR, prefix=".probe.")
        try:
            os.write(fd, b"x" * 4096)
            os.fsync(fd)
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
            os.unlink(probe)
        return True
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"nothing sent: the state folder {STATE_DIR} is not writable ({e.strerror})")
        return False


def main():
    state = read_state()
    view, reason = check(set(state["defaults"]))
    if view is None:
        now = "broken:" + sha(reason)
        log(f"the check could not run: {escape_invisible(reason)}")
        if state["check"] == now:
            log("the check is broken; already reported, nothing sent")
            return 1
        if not writable():
            return 1
        eid = send(BROKEN_TEXT)
        if eid is None:
            log("the check is broken; will try again next run")
            return 1
        state["check"] = now
        write_state(state)
        log("the check is broken; reported")
        mark_open(eid)   # guard:markopen
        return 1
    c = view["classes"]
    log(f"rules not on record: {len(c['unmade'])}; rules on record that are gone: {len(c['gone'])}; "
        f"expired but still live: {len(c['expired'])}")
    if not writable():
        return 1
    # The lists "$defaults" is live in now, kept (as list names only) so its
    # removal is asked about even where the snapshot never held it.
    seen = {k for k in KEYS if DEFAULTS in view["live"][k]}
    if not seen <= set(state["defaults"]):
        state["defaults"] = sorted(set(state["defaults"]) | seen)
        write_state(state)
    view, answered = answer(state, view)
    if view is None:
        return 1
    posted, asked = ask(state, view)
    if state["check"] != "ok":
        if posted or send(CLEAR_TEXT) is not None:
            state["check"] = "ok"
            write_state(state)
            log("the check runs again")
        else:
            asked = False
    if not posted and not state["alerts"]:
        log("nothing to ask about")
    return 0 if answered and asked else 1


def report_failure(e):
    """Anything the guard did not plan for is a broken check, reported once,
    never a run that ends silently. The log names the error's type only: its
    text could carry what the guard read."""
    reason = f"the guard failed ({type(e).__name__})"
    log(f"the check could not run: {reason}")
    try:
        try:
            state = read_state()
        except Exception:   # the state itself may be what failed: report from nothing on record
            state = fresh_state()
        now = "broken:" + sha(reason)
        if state["check"] == now:
            log("the check is broken; already reported, nothing sent")
        elif writable():
            eid = send(BROKEN_TEXT)
            if eid is not None:
                state["check"] = now
                write_state(state)
                log("the check is broken; reported")
                mark_open(eid)   # guard:markopen
    except Exception as e2:
        log(f"the failure could not be reported either ({type(e2).__name__})")
    return 1


try:
    code_ = main()
except Exception as e:
    code_ = report_failure(e)
sys.exit(code_)
